39: 【132†RELEASING.md】  | 【132†RELEASING.md】  |    |\nL240: 【133†cliff.toml】  | 【133†cliff.toml】  |    |\nL241: 【134†create_release.sh】  | 【134†create_release.sh】  |    |\nL242: 【135†release-plz.toml】  | 【135†release-plz.toml】  |    |\nL243: 【136†rustfmt.toml】  | 【136†rustfmt.toml】  |    |\nL244: [Button: View all files]\nL245: ## Repository files navigation\nL246: \nL247:   *   * 【137†README】\nL248:   * 【137†Contributing】\nL249:   * 【137†Apache-2.0 license】\nL250:   * 【137†MIT license】\nL251: \nL252: More items\nL253: \nL254: Plonky3 is a toolkit which provides a set of primitives, such as polynomial commitment schemes, for implementing polynomial IOPs (PIOPs). It is mainly used to power STARK-based zkVMs, though in principle it may be used for PLONK-based circuits or other PIOPs.\nL255: \nL256: For questions or discussions, please use the Telegram group, 【138†t.me/plonky3†t.me】.\nL257: ## Deprecated crates\nL258: \nL259: Starting with v0.9.0, the following crates are deprecated and planned for removal in a future release:\nL260: \nL261:   * `p3-bn254`\nL262:   * `p3-monolith`\nL263:   * `p3-monolith-air`\nL264:   * `p3-poseidon1-air`\nL265:   * `p3-poseidon2-air`\nL266:   * `p3-rescue`\nL267: \nL268: Avoid using these crates in new projects. A removal version has not yet been scheduled.\nL269: ## Status\nL270: \nL271: Fields:\nL272:   * [Input] Mersenne31\nL273:     * [Input] \"complex\" extension field\nL274:     * [Input] ~128 bit extension field\nL275:     * [Input] AVX2\nL276:     * [Input] AVX-512\nL277:     * [Input] NEON\nL278:   * [Input] General 31 bit fields (BabyBear and KoalaBear)\nL279:     * [Input] ~128 bit extension field\nL280:     * [Input] AVX2\nL281:     * [Input] AVX-512\nL282:     * [Input] NEON\nL283:   * [Input] Goldilocks\nL284:     * [Input] ~128 bit extension field\nL285:   * [Input] Binary tower fields GF(2) ⊂ GF(4) ⊂ … ⊂ GF(2^128)\nL286:     * [Input] GHASH polynomial-basis representation of GF(2^128), with SIMD packings\nL287: Generalized vector commitment schemes\nL288: \nL289:   * [Input] generalized Merkle tree\nL290: \nL291: Polynomial commitment schemes\nL292: \nL293:   * [Input] FRI-based PCS\nL294:   * [Input] STIR-based PCS\nL295:   * [Input] WHIR-based PCS\nL296:   * [Input] Multilinear PCS over binary tower fields (Binius-style, additive-domain proximity)\nL297:   * [Input] tensor PCS\nL298:   * [Input] univariate-to-multivariate adapter\nL299:   * [Input] multivariate-to-univariate adapter\nL300: \nL301: PIOPs\nL302:   * [Input] univariate STARK\nL303:   * [Input] multivariate STARK (SuperSpartan-style, over AIRs)\nL304:   * [Input] batched STARK (shared PCS openings across multiple instances)\nL305:   * [Input] PLONK\nL306: \nL307: Interactive proof primitives\nL308: \nL309:   * [Input] Sumcheck (generic-degree, small-value optimization, HVZK masking, ring switching)\nL310:   * [Input] LogUp lookup arguments (local and cross-AIR)\nL311:   * [Input] Typed Fiat-Shamir transcript layer (DSFS / IETF draft)\nL312: \nL313: Security\nL396: `test-util`  | `p3-sumcheck` (off by default)  | Compiles the sumcheck test helper module used by downstream tests.\nL397: There is no `nightly`, `asm`, or `simd` Cargo feature — SIMD code paths are gated by `cfg(target_feature = …)` and enabled through `RUSTFLAGS`, not features. See 【144†CPU features】 above.\nL398: ### Recommended `RUSTFLAGS`\nL399:   * `-Ctarget-cpu=native` — enables every SIMD instruction set the host supports (AVX2 / AVX-512 / NEON / SIMD128 fallbacks for Mersenne31, BabyBear, KoalaBear, Goldilocks). Required to get parity with the published benchmark numbers.\nL400:   * `lto = \"fat\"` in `[profile.release]` of the workspace `Cargo.toml` (or your downstream) for a some release-mode speedup, at the cost of longer compile times.\nL401:   * `JEMALLOC_SYS_WITH_MALLOC_CONF=retain:true,dirty_decay_ms:-1,muzzy_decay_ms:-1` for repeated-proof workloads (see 【145†Benchmarks】).\nL402: ## Known issues\nL403: \nL404:   * The verifier may panic on certain malformed proofs. Downstream integrators that accept untrusted proofs should wrap `verify` in `catch_unwind` out of caution.\nL405: \nL406: ## License\nL407: \nL408: Licensed under either of\nL409: \nL410:   * Apache License, Version 2.0, (【129†LICENSE-APACHE】 or 【146†http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0†www.apache.org】)\nL411:   * MIT license (【130†LICENSE-MIT】 or 【147†http://opensource.org/licenses/MIT†opensource.org】)\nL412: \nL413: at your option.\nL414: ## Guidance for external contributors\nL415: \nL416: Do you feel keen and able to help with Plonky3? That's great! We encourage external contributions!\nL417: \nL418: We want to make it easy for you to contribute, but at the same time, we must manage the burden of reviewing external contributions. We are a small team, and the time we spend reviewing external contributions is time we are not developing ourselves.\nL419: We also want to help you avoid inadvertently duplicating work that is already underway, or building something that we will not want to incorporate.\nL420: \nL421: First and foremost, please keep in mind that this is a highly technical piece of software and contributing is only suitable for experienced mathematicians, cryptographers, and software engineers.\nL422: The Polygon Zero Team reserves the right to accept or reject any external contribution for any reason, including a simple lack of time to maintain it (now or in the future); we may even decline to review something that is not considered a sufficiently high priority for us.\nL423: To avoid disappointment, please communicate your intention to contribute openly, while respecting the limited time and availability we have to review and provide guidance for external contributions. It is a good idea to drop a note in our public Discord #development channel about your intention to work on something, whether an issue, a new feature, or a performance improvement. This is probably all that's really required to avoid duplication of work with other contributors.\nL424: What follows are some more specific requests for how to write PRs in a way that will make them easy for us to review. Deviating from these guidelines may result in your PR being rejected, ignored, or forgotten.\nL425: ### General guidance for your PR\nL426: \nL427: Obviously, PRs will not be considered unless they pass our GitHub CI. The GitHub CI is not executed for PRs from forks, but you can run its routine host checks locally with `python3 scripts/check.py full`; see 【127†CONTRIBUTING.md】 for targeted and cross-platform commands.\nL428: Under no circumstances should a single PR mix different purposes: Your PR is either a bug fix, a new feature, or a performance improvement, never a combination. Nor should you include, for example, two unrelated performance improvements in one PR. Please just submit separate PRs. The goal is to make reviewing your PR as simple as possible, and you should be thinking about how to compose the PR to minimize the burden on the reviewer.\nL429: Plonky3 uses stable Rust, so any PR that depends on unstable features is likely to be rejected. It's possible that we may relax this policy in the future, but we aim to minimize the use of unstable features; please discuss with us before enabling any.\nL430: \nL431: Please do not submit any PRs consisting of merely minor fixes to documentation. They will not be accepted. If you find something that bothers you particularly, make an issue and we will fix it when we find the time.\nL433: #### The PR fixes a bug\nL434: \nL435: In the PR description, please clearly but briefly describe\nL436: \nL437:   1. the bug (could be a reference to a GH issue; if it is from a discussion (on Discord/email/etc. for example), please copy in the relevant parts of the discussion);\nL438:   2. what turned out to cause the bug; and\nL439:   3. how the PR fixes the bug.\nL440: \nL441: Wherever possible, PRs that fix bugs should include additional tests that (i) trigger the original bug and (ii) pass after applying the PR.\nL442: #### The PR implements a new feature\nL443: \nL444: If you plan to contribute the implementation of a new feature, please double-check with the Polygon Zero team that it is a sufficient priority for us that it will be reviewed and integrated.\nL445: \nL446: In the PR description, please clearly but briefly describe\nL447: \nL448:   1. what the feature does\nL449:   2. the approach taken to implement it\nL450: \nL451: All PRs for new features must include a suitable test suite.\nL452: #### The PR improves performance\nL453: \nL454: Performance improvements are particularly welcome! Please note that it can be quite difficult to establish true improvements for the workloads we care about. To help filter out false positives, the PR description for a performance improvement must clearly identify\nL455:   1. the target bottleneck (only one per PR to avoid confusing things!)\nL456:   2. how performance is measured\nL457:   3. characteristics of the machine used (CPU, OS, #threads if appropriate)\nL458:   4. performance before and after the PR\nL459: ### Licensing\nL460: \nL461: Unless you explicitly state otherwise, any contribution intentionally submitted for inclusion in the work by you, as defined in the Apache-2.0 license, shall be dual licensed as above, without any additional terms or conditions.\nL462: \nL463: ## About\nL464: \nL465: A toolkit for polynomial IOPs (PIOPs)\nL466: \nL467: ### Resources\nL468: \nL469: 【57†Readme】\nL470: \nL471: Apache-2.0, MIT licenses found\nL472: \nL473: ### Contributing\nL474: \nL475: 【58†Contributing】\nL476: \nL477: 【59†Activity】\nL478: \nL479: 【60†Custom properties】\nL480: \nL481: ### Stars\nL482: \nL483: 862 stars\nL484: \nL485: ### Watchers\nL486: \nL487: 23 watching\nL488: ### Forks\nL489: \nL490: 【61†478 forks】\nL491: \nL492: 【62†Report repository】\nL493: \nL494: ## Used by\nL495: \nL496: ## Contributors\nL497: \nL498: ## Languages\nL499: \nL500: You can’t perform that action at th